endpoint: root:@tcp(10.2.12.57:32334)/
date_utc_seen_by_tidb: 2026-07-10
purpose: target-analysis for target.source.dynamic-state-ingress.pkg-executor-show.v1

SQL shape:
  DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS ai_state_show_probe;
  CREATE DATABASE ai_state_show_probe;
  USE ai_state_show_probe;
  CREATE TABLE t(id INT PRIMARY KEY, v INT);
  INSERT INTO t VALUES (1,10);
  SET @stale_ts = DATE_FORMAT(NOW(6), '%Y-%m-%d %H:%i:%s.%f');
  DO SLEEP(0.2);
  INSERT INTO t VALUES (2,20);
  SELECT 'asof', GROUP_CONCAT(id ORDER BY id) FROM t AS OF TIMESTAMP @stale_ts;
  SELECT 'current', GROUP_CONCAT(id ORDER BY id) FROM t;
  SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY AS OF TIMESTAMP @stale_ts;
  SHOW TABLE STATUS LIKE 't';
  SELECT 'after_show', GROUP_CONCAT(id ORDER BY id) FROM t;
  SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY AS OF TIMESTAMP @stale_ts;
  SELECT 'direct_after_set', GROUP_CONCAT(id ORDER BY id) FROM t;

Observed output:
  ts                 2026-07-10 02:54:34.003320
  asof               1
  current            1,2
  SHOW TABLE STATUS  returned row for t
  after_show         1,2
  direct_after_set   1

Interpretation:
  SHOW TABLE STATUS does consume the pending SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY AS OF TIMESTAMP state.
  This is live, user-visible behavior on the supplied testbed, but it is not enough to file a bug.
  The product contract must decide whether SHOW TABLE STATUS counts as the next query statement.
  If SHOW is a query statement, consumption is expected. If SET TRANSACTION is intended only for
  the next user data SELECT/execute statement, this is a semantic-gray state-ingress candidate.
